## THE TWO STATISTICS DISAGREE IN DIRECTION THIS TIME — which is the real finding

                          before      after      change
    candidate p50 (ms)   17.540564   16.778213   -4.35%
    live scipy p50 (ms)  11.698059   11.716650   +0.16%   <- host control, essentially flat
    median-of-ratios       0.713729    0.700061   -1.92%   <- harness statistic: WORSE
    ratio-of-medians       0.666915    0.698325   +4.71%   <- cruder view: BETTER

Last time these two agreed in direction and differed 4x in magnitude. This time they disagree
in SIGN. The harness's paired statistic says the lever made things worse; the ratio of the two
medians says it made things better by 4.71%. Both cannot be informative about a lever whose
COUNTED effect is -2.46% of a kernel inside the factor, i.e. well under 0.5% of the job.

And the raw candidate move (-4.35%) is itself an order of magnitude larger than the counted
prediction, on a run where the live arm did not move at all (+0.16%). A 4.35% shift in our arm
with a flat incumbent arm is not something a 0.5%-of-job change can produce. Something other
than this lever moved between the two runs, which is exactly what an ACROSS-RUN comparison
cannot control for and exactly what the failed control null is reporting.

## WHAT THIS ROW ESTABLISHES

Nothing about the outer-walk lever, in either direction. It does not confirm the counted result
and it does not falsify it. The prediction registered BEFORE the run — that the effect is below
this cell's resolution and an in-floor or noise-dominated result was expected — is what
happened, and the failed null plus the sign disagreement are the mechanism.

THE COUNTED ROW REMAINS THE EVIDENCE for this lever: 18,194 -> 17,297 Ir per call, deterministic,
one process, no host dependence (commit 6c147ffd6).

WHAT WOULD MAKE AN END-TO-END ROW MEANINGFUL HERE: not a quieter host — the previous run had all
four nulls clean and still could not separate a change this size. It needs the two kernels
alternated ABBA inside ONE window behind a toggle, which is the same instrument gap I flagged for
the inner-loop lever. Until that exists, sub-1%-of-job kernel changes on this cell are not
end-to-end measurable and should be decided on counts.

